course: money
lesson: 8
title: Saving up for a game
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Saving means putting money aside, week after week, until you have enough for something bigger than one week's money can buy.
explain: Sam wants a £24 game and gets £3 a week: £24 ÷ £3 = 8 weeks, and knowing the number makes the wait feel shorter.
explain: If you already have some money, take it away from the price first, then divide what is left by what you save each week: Arlo has £20 towards an £80 bike and saves £5 a week, so £60 ÷ £5 = 12 weeks.
explain: Spending on something else on the way sets your goal back, and waiting can help: prices often drop when a newer version comes out, and a game you waited for usually feels extra special.
fact: Saving means keeping money aside for later instead of spending it now.
fact: A saving goal (or target) is the thing or amount you are saving for.
fact: To find how many weeks: take what you already have from the price, then divide by what you save each week.
fact: Borrowing means taking money you must pay back; money you owe is called a debt.
fact: An impulse buy is buying something suddenly without planning, and it can set your saving goal back.
fact: Some parents or employers add money to what you save; this is called matching, like a reward for saving.
fact: A chart, jar or app that shows your progress helps you keep going.
fact: Prices often drop when a newer version comes out, so waiting can save money.
fact: Waiting for something better later is called delayed gratification.
fact: Counting your savings: group the coins, multiply, then add the groups.
fact: Most people do a mix of spending some now and saving some for later.
exercise: Sam wants a £24 game and saves £3 a week. How many weeks will it take? | 8 | 6 | 7 | 21
exercise: Freya has £15 for a £20 Lego set. How much more does she need? | £5 | £15 | £35 | £10
exercise: Freya saves £2.50 a week. How many weeks to get the £5 she needs? | 2 | 5 | 3 | 1
exercise: Why might Freya wait instead of borrowing a fiver from Mum? | She won't owe anyone money, so there's no debt to pay back | Borrowing is against the law | Mum's money is fake | Waiting makes the Lego cheaper
exercise: Kai had saved £15 for £30 headphones, then spent £10 on a hat. How much does he have now? | £5 | £15 | £20 | £10
exercise: After the hat, how much more does Kai need for the £30 headphones? | £25 | £15 | £20 | £30
exercise: What is it called when you buy something suddenly without planning? | An impulse buy | A saving goal reached | A matching deal | A debt to pay back
exercise: Lucy's mum adds 50p for every £1 Lucy saves. Lucy saves £10. How much does she have in all? | £15 | £10.50 | £20 | £12
exercise: Arlo wants an £80 bike. He has £20 and saves £5 a week. How many weeks? | 12 | 16 | 4 | 10
exercise: Mia saves £2 a week and colours a box for every £1. Her goal is £20. After 7 weeks, how many boxes are left? | 6 | 14 | 13 | 7
exercise: Ben saved £4 a week for 10 weeks. How much did he save? | £40 | £14 | £44 | £36
exercise: Ali's game costs £40 now and might be £25 next month. How much might he save by waiting? | £15 | £25 | £65 | £10
exercise: Gracie has four £2 coins, six £1 coins and five 20p coins. How much is that? | £15 | £13 | £16 | £12
exercise: Paddy saves half of £5 every week for a year (52 weeks). How much does he save? | £130 | £260 | £104 | £52
exercise: Why do prices often drop when a new version of a game comes out? | People want the new one, so the old one is sold cheaper | The old one stops working | The shops lose the old discs | It is the law to halve old prices
exercise: Why can saving up make a game feel more special? | You thought about it a lot, so you know you really wanted it | Saved money buys better copies of the game | The shop gives it a gold box | It is always cheaper after saving
test: Sam groans "how long's that gonna take?" — game's £24, he gets £3 a week, and Gran's just given him £6. Now how many weeks? | 6 | 8 | 7 | 10
test: Paddy goes "saving's for losers, spend it now lol". His sis rolls her eyes. What's the sensible view? | A mix is best: spend some now, save some for bigger things | He's right, saving never helps | Saving everything and never spending is the only way | Spending everything is the same as saving
test: Kai saw "a well cool hat" halfway to his headphones and bought it. His dad says "that's fine, if...?" | ...he chose it on purpose and knows the goal is further away now | ...the hat was more than £30 | ...he borrows the rest from a friend | ...he never wears the hat
test: Arlo says "I've got £20, bike's £80, I save a fiver a week, so 16 weeks, sorted." What did he forget? | To take away the £20 he already has, so it's 12 weeks | To add the £20, so it's 20 weeks | Nothing, 16 weeks is right | That bikes cost £100
test: Lucy goes "wicked!" ("wicked" is British slang for great) at Mum's 50p-per-pound deal. She saves £8. How much does Mum add? | £4 | £8 | £2 | £12
test: What would happen if Mia stopped colouring her chart? | She might lose track and feel less like keeping going | Her money would vanish from the jar | The goal would get bigger | Nothing could ever change
test: Ali's mate: "new version next month, so the old one'll be dirt cheap." ("Dirt cheap" means very cheap.) What's the trade-off? | Wait and pay less, or pay more now and play sooner | There's no trade-off at all, because the old one is free | The new one will cost less than the old one | Waiting always costs more
test: Gracie tips her jar out: "four £2s, six quid coins — no hang on, seven — and five 20ps." What's the total? | £16 | £15 | £17 | £14
test: A Yorkshire grandad says "nowt comes to them as won't wait, lad" ("nowt" means nothing). Which money idea is that? | Saving and waiting for something bigger | Spending everything straight away on treats | Borrowing from everyone you know at school | Buying on impulse whenever you see a sale
test: Ben saved £40 over 10 weeks and the game was £35. How much did he have left? | £5 | £15 | £75 | £0
test: Freya's bro says "just borrow a fiver off Mum, duh." If she does, what happens? | She owes Mum £5, which is a debt to pay back | She gets £5 free forever and never has to pay it back | Mum pays the shop and Freya gets the change to keep | The Lego becomes £5 cheaper because Mum helped
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the act of saving? | The setting aside of money for subsequent use in place of immediate expenditure | The acquisition of goods without prior planning | The borrowing of money from a relation | The exchange of money for goods of equal value
exam: It is given that a bicycle is priced at eighty pounds, that twenty pounds is already held, and that five pounds is saved weekly. Determine the number of weeks required. | Twelve | Sixteen | Four | Ten
exam: It is given that a parent contributes fifty pence for each pound saved, and that ten pounds is saved. Ascertain the aggregate sum thereupon held. | Fifteen pounds | Ten pounds fifty pence | Twenty pounds | Twelve pounds
exam: Identify the principal effect of an unplanned purchase upon progress towards a saving goal. | The attainment of the goal is deferred | The goal is attained more rapidly than planned | The price of the goal is reduced in consequence | No effect whatsoever is had upon the goal
